    "updated_at": "2020-08-06T10:37:58Z",
    "body": "Huh. I was not aware of this ticket, but Firefox didn't use to cope with iframes and web-based handlers at all, always opening a new tab/window. When I fixed this in Firefox 78, I [added the functionality requested in the initial comment here](https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D74560): we open web-based protocol handlers that aren't using the scheme prefix `web+` (or `ext+` from extensions) outside of the iframe (I opted for the equivalent of `_blank` as @annevk 's comment suggested). I did this precisely because of the x-frame-options issue that was highlighted in the first comment -- before my changes, this was already what would happen (as we didn't support opening web-based links in frames at all) and I didn't want to regress this.\r\n\r\nI still implemented Blink's behaviour (ie not doing anything special and trying to open in the frame to which the link pointed) for the `web+` and `ext+` cases because I assumed that (due to them effectively not being standardized and never being handled by external applications like `mailto:` is) there should be more of an expectation from the website that they open in whatever frame required, as was the original complaint in the [bug where this got changed](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1196151#c0).",
