    "created_at": "2021-04-26T06:44:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-04-26T06:44:14Z",
    "body": "Hmmmm interesting callback.\r\n\r\n> @mgiuca since you filed that bug, thoughts?\r\n\r\n(referring to [this Firefox bug](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1196151))\r\n\r\nSo I do remember exactly what I was trying to do in 2015 at the time I filed that bug. As I said in [the comment](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1196151#c2), I was trying to essentially implement a hack way of doing a primitive form of web intents that I learned about from a blog post, because I was trying to prototype [Ballista](https://github.com/chromium/ballista) which eventually turned into [Web Share Target](https://w3c.github.io/web-share-target/). So there's some storied history here :)\r\n\r\nBut I don't think I was specifically asking for `web+` schemes to behave differently to other schemes. I think they should all behave the same way.\r\n\r\nThe thing I was trying to do in that bug was a rather obscure hack, and I don't think it should govern what is the \"correct\" behaviour.\r\n\r\nIt does seem pretty weird to open the handler inside an iframe, and as @ericlaw1979 writes in the OP, has a bunch of issues. So I would prefer to just change the behaviour (if it's an option) to always open in a top-level frame.",
